!***************************************************************
!This is a simple one-zone collapse test following
! the chemical and thermal evolution of a metal-enriched cloud
! (solar metallicity) with CO and H2O freeze-out and evaporation
! on dust, the tabulated dust temperature, dust cooling and H2
! formation on dust (legacy dust tables, MC field).
!The dynamics is described by the Larson-Penston-type
! similar solution and includes cooling and heating processes.
!For additional details look also to Omukai 2005.
!(legacy tests/collapseCO had no README; text from its test.f90)
!***************************************************************

KROME-X port (examples/tests/collapseCO, driver test.cpp on the C++ API):
- network: COthin_ice_dustH2.knet in this directory = networks/
  COthin_ice.knet plus H + {H} -> H2 with krate_H2dust_table(). Legacy
  -dustTabs=H2 added that H2 formation on dust to the ODEs; in KROME-X it is a
  network reaction (the legacy option stops with that message);
- legacy -dustTabs=H2,COOL,MC = dust.tables = "MC" (Tdust, the H2 table) +
  thermal.cooling = ["...", "DUSTTAB"] (the COOL table);
- CI,CII,OI line cooling = thermal.cooling = ["Z"] thermal.metals = ["C", "C+", "O"] (translated);
- dropped: -maxord=1 (CVODE's BDF order), -photoDustVarAv=user_av (Av is
  the per-cell parameter Av, set by the driver at each step as legacy),
  -useTabs (rate tables retired);
- parameters: legacy user_crate / user_Av are p.crate / p.Av; the legacy
  PHOTODUST heating hardcoded the Draine field G = 1.69 exp(-2.5 Av): the
  driver sets p.G0 = 1.69; the legacy CMB temperature 2.73 K is p.Tcmb
  (KROME-X default 2.725);
- the tabulated Tdust written in abundances.dat (legacy krome_get_table_Tdust)
  is the network's table_Tdust(Tgas, n, p), which is not a public query;
- abundances.dat now has a header line (#ntot Tgas Tdust <species>, for
  plot.py); the ices are species of their own (CO_ice, H2O_ice) where legacy
  wrote the totals CO_total, H2O_total (ice = total - gas); plot.py plots them;
- numerics: KROME-X default tolerances (rtol 1e-8; legacy 1e-4, -ATOL=1d-40 is
  the species atol_n); at n ~ 1e12 the gas cools from ~1e9 K to ~12 K
  within one step: CVODE fails there once, and its restart continues from
  a smaller first step;
- physics, as legacy: with cosmic-ray heating and no ATOMIC cooling the
  gas runs away to ~1e10 K up to n ~ 1e11 cm^-3, then the dust cooling
  brings it to Tdust (~12-29 K). KROME-X follows legacy within ~1% (median)
  in Tgas along the whole track, and in Tdust wherever Tgas < 1e9 K
  (1e-4 in the cold phase). Above 1e9 K the written Tdust differs (up to
  x2.6): KROME-X's get_Tdust reports the dust temperature the rates see, at
  the rate temperature capped at 1e9 K (both codes' rates are capped
  there), while legacy's krome_get_table_Tdust evaluates the table at the
  raw Tgas, extrapolating it far beyond its grid. It is a difference of
  the diagnostic only;
- differences: legacy kept only the last "CO_ice -> CO" rate of the
  network (the non-thermal evaporation) and dropped the thermal
  evaporation 1e12 exp(-1100/Tdust); KROME-X keeps both, so gas-phase CO
  evaporates once Tdust > ~20 K (n > 1e14: CO/ntot 2.7e-8 at 1e16 cm^-3
  vs legacy 6e-17). The KROME-X PHOTOAV heating is not generated for this
  network (codegen: "heating_photoAv stubbed: needs a Solomon-process
  H2 -> H + H reaction"; legacy used the first H2 -> H + H rate, the
  5.6e-11 exp(-3.74 Av) fshield photodissociation); it is negligible
  along this track;
- plot: python3 plot.py (plot.png).
- 2.0.0: thermal.gamma = "EXACT" (every diatomic with its exact rotation and
  vibration); legacy -gamma=REDUCED (H2 and CO only, no vibration) was
  removed. Against the v2.0.0-benchmark run (REDUCED, and the legacy
  physical constants, now CODATA 2018): Tgas within 6e-6 (median); 2.6 %
  at most, in 7 rows of the steep rise to 1e7 K at 4e11 cm^-3 (the rise
  shifts slightly). The legacy comparison of this test is that of
  v2.0.0-benchmark (bench/results/benchmark).
- 2.0.0: the C, C+ and O line cooling of coolZ.dat uses current collision
  data (Lique et al. 2018 corrected for O; Klos et al. 2021 and Barinovs
  et al. 2005 for C+; Yan & Babb 2023 for C + H). Tgas moves by 7e-4
  (median); 67 of the 3933 rows change by more than 5 %, at the start and
  in the steep transitions of the track.
- output files (legacy name): abundances.dat (fort.22).
